# Who may do what

```elixir
Mix.install([{:wardrik, "~> 0.1.0-rc.2"}, {:kino, "~> 0.19"}])
```

## What you'll build

By the end, Maria owns an agent, Jon can read it but not stop it, and Sam can't even see it.
Then you make an API token, use it, and revoke it. Nothing here calls a model, so you need no key.

## Setup

Start Wardrik, act as Maria, and define her agent:

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Wardrik.start_link(name: MyApp.AgentRuntime)

scope = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@maria", roles: [:owner], resources: ["acme/@maria/*"])

{:ok, _agent} =
  Wardrik.Agent.define(scope, "acme/@maria/books",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",
    instructions: "You keep Maria's books."
  )
```

A scope is three things: Wardrik, who acts, and what they're granted. Every operation takes one.
Maria is `:owner` of everything under `acme/@maria/`.

## Two more people

Jon may read and message Maria's agents. Sam has no grants at all:

```elixir
jon =
  Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@jon",
    roles: [:reader, :messenger],
    resources: ["acme/@maria/agent/*"]
  )

sam = Wardrik.as(MyApp.AgentRuntime, "acme/@sam")
```

A resource names what's acted on. The agent `acme/@maria/books` is the resource
`acme/@maria/agent/books`.

## Try it

Each person tries the same four things on Maria's agent:

```elixir
people = [maria: scope, jon: jon, sam: sam]

tries = [
  read: &Wardrik.Agent.read(&1, "acme/@maria/books"),
  events: &Wardrik.Agent.events(&1, "acme/@maria/books"),
  stop: &Wardrik.Agent.stop(&1, "acme/@maria/books"),
  define: &Wardrik.Agent.define(&1, "acme/@maria/books", model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5")
]

answer = fn
  {:error, %Wardrik.Error{type: type}} -> Atom.to_string(type)
  _ok -> "ok"
end

may = fn people ->
  for {name, person} <- people do
    Map.new([{:person, name} | for({action, try} <- tries, do: {action, answer.(try.(person))})])
  end
  |> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: [:person | Keyword.keys(tries)], name: "Who may do what")
end

may.(people)
```

* Maria may do all of it.
* Jon may read, but `stop` and `define` are `forbidden`. A statement reaches the agent, without
  the action.
* Sam gets `not_found` for everything. No statement reaches the agent, so for him it isn't there.

The two errors read differently:

```elixir
{Wardrik.Agent.stop(jon, "acme/@maria/books"), Wardrik.Agent.read(sam, "acme/@maria/books")}
```

`not_found` keeps Sam from learning the agent exists.

## Grant one more thing

`Wardrik.grant/2` adds a statement to a scope. Let Jon define this one agent:

```elixir
jon = Wardrik.grant(jon, roles: [:definer], resources: ["acme/@maria/agent/books"])

may.(maria: scope, jon: jon, sam: sam)
```

Jon may now stop and define `books`. His grant names one agent, so Maria's other agents stay as
they were.

| Role | Actions |
| --- | --- |
| `:reader` | `agent.read`, `agent.events`, `connection.read`, `mcp_server.read`, `ceiling.read` |
| `:messenger` | `agent.ask`, `agent.cancel`, `agent.clear` |
| `:approver` | `agent.decide` |
| `:definer` | `agent.define`, `agent.spawn`, `agent.stop`, `agent.forget`, `connection.connect`, `mcp_server.add` |
| `:worker` | `agent.serve` |
| `:owner` | every action |

## API tokens

Programs reach Wardrik over HTTP with a token. Making tokens needs `deployment.tokens` on
`deployment`:

```elixir
admin = Wardrik.grant(scope, actions: ["deployment.tokens"], resources: ["deployment"])

{:ok, issued} =
  Wardrik.Tokens.create(admin,
    name: "ci",
    roles: ["reader", "messenger"],
    resources: ["acme/@maria/agent/*"],
    expires_in: 3600
  )

issued.secret
```

The secret is returned once, now. Wardrik keeps only its SHA-256.

The token acts for Maria. Turn its secret back into a scope, and use it:

```elixir
{:ok, ci} = Wardrik.authenticate(MyApp.AgentRuntime, issued.secret)

{ci.principal.id, answer.(Wardrik.Agent.read(ci, "acme/@maria/books")),
 answer.(Wardrik.Agent.stop(ci, "acme/@maria/books"))}
```

It may read, as `reader` allows. It may not stop the agent.

## Make tokens from a form

A token can't do more than whoever makes it. Pick a role and the resources it reaches. The table
below lists every token:

```elixir
tokens = Kino.Frame.new()

show_tokens = fn ->
  {:ok, list} = Wardrik.Tokens.list(admin)

  rows =
    for token <- list do
      %{
        id: token.id,
        name: token.name,
        for: token.principal,
        actions: Enum.flat_map(token.statements, & &1.actions) |> Enum.join(", "),
        resources: Enum.flat_map(token.statements, & &1.resources) |> Enum.uniq() |> Enum.join(", "),
        expires: token.expires
      }
    end

  Kino.Frame.render(
    tokens,
    Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: [:id, :name, :for, :actions, :resources, :expires], name: "Tokens")
  )
end

form =
  Kino.Control.form(
    [
      name: Kino.Input.text("Name", default: "deploy-bot"),
      role: Kino.Input.select("Role", [{"definer", "definer"}, {"reader", "reader"}, {"owner", "owner"}]),
      resources: Kino.Input.text("Resources", default: "acme/@maria/agent/*")
    ],
    submit: "Make token"
  )

result = Kino.Frame.new()

make_token = fn %{data: %{name: name, role: role, resources: resources}} ->
  case Wardrik.Tokens.create(admin, name: name, roles: [role], resources: [resources]) do
    {:ok, issued} ->
      Kino.Frame.render(result, Kino.Markdown.new("Made **#{name}**. Its secret: `#{issued.secret}`"))

    {:error, error} ->
      Kino.Frame.render(result, Kino.Markdown.new("**#{error.type}**: #{error.message}"))
  end

  show_tokens.()
end

Kino.listen(form, make_token)
show_tokens.()

Kino.Layout.grid([form, result, tokens], columns: 1)
```

Try `acme/agent/*` as the resources. Maria owns `acme/@maria/*` alone, so that token is
`forbidden`: it would reach agents she can't.

## Revoke a token

A revoked token is refused from then on:

```elixir
{:ok, _token} = Wardrik.Tokens.revoke(admin, issued.token)
show_tokens.()

Wardrik.authenticate(MyApp.AgentRuntime, issued.secret)
```

The `ci` row leaves the table. Agents a token defined keep running, under the policy they were
defined with.

